RUN END: 2026-10-15 21:21 NZDT (2026-10-15 08:21 UTC)

VERDICT: PASS -- a secondary IP address on the x960-56YSQ is absent by default, and once
configured it genuinely SERVES TRAFFIC rather than merely appearing in a show command: the DUT
answers ARP for it with its own MAC (4/4, against 0/4 with the feature off), installs a
connected route for its subnet, and forwards traffic arriving from another subnet INTO it (5/5
on the wire, against 0/5 at baseline) while an unconfigured control subnet is dropped in the
same leg.  The DUT uses the secondary as its own interface identity when it ARPs for a host
there (`tell 172.16.10.1`).  Removal takes the address out of the dataplane as well as the
config (ARP 4 -> 0, forwarding 5 -> 0), and everything survives a real reboot.  The two
documented ordering rules are enforced on hardware with clear messages: `% Configure primary
first vlan99` and `% Must delete secondary before deleting primary` -- so removing the primary
is REFUSED, not cascaded, and nothing is silently lost.  Two observations: `show ip interface
brief` shows only the primary (O-3), and the DUT answers ARP for an address on the interface
only when the ARP sender is inside THAT address's subnet -- symmetric for primary and secondary,
which is what confirms the guide's "treated the same" claim rather than contradicting it (F-4).
One planned discriminator was discarded at baseline because it did not discriminate: a frame
merely SOURCED in the secondary subnet crosses the DUT with the feature off too, since
forwarding is destination-based and there is no uRPF.
